## Fixed-Size Arrays

An **array** `[N]T` has a fixed size known at compile time. Unlike Vec, arrays live on the stack and can't grow or shrink.

### Declaring Arrays

```xiom
let a: [3]Int = [1, 2, 3];
let b = [4, 5, 6];  // type inferred: [3]Int
```

The size `N` is part of the type — `[3]Int` and `[4]Int` are different types.

### Accessing Elements

```xiom
let arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];

let first = arr[0];   // 10
let last = arr[4];    // 50
let len = arr.len();  // 5

let safe = arr.get(0);   // Some(10)
let out = arr.get(10);   // None
```

### Arrays vs Vec

| Feature | Array [N]T | Vec[T] |
|---------|-----------|--------|
| Size | Fixed at compile time | Grows at runtime |
| Memory | Stack | Heap |
| Push/Pop | Not available | Available |
| Indexing | Bracket syntax | Bracket syntax |
| Copy | Copy (if elements are Copy) | Move |

### When to Use Arrays

- Small, fixed-size collections (days of week, directions)
- Performance-critical code (stack allocation faster than heap)
- Embedded systems with limited heap

```xiom
pub type Board = [9]Int;  // tic-tac-toe board

fn empty_board() -> Board {
  return [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0];
}

fn make_move(board: &mut Board, pos: Int, player: Int) -> Unit {
  board[pos] = player;
}
```

### Array Iteration

```xiom
let arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
var sum = 0;
var i = 0;
while i < arr.len() {
  sum = sum + arr[i];
  i = i + 1;
}
```

